<header><p class="st-kicker">theocracy · China, Tibet and Mongolia</p><h1 class="st-title">Sakya regime</h1><p class="st-meta">1268 CE – 1354 CE (approx.) · capital Sakya</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The first Tibetan government under Mongol patronage, in which the abbots of Sakya monastery and their administrators ruled Tibet for the Yuan emperors.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>In 1244 the scholar <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sakya-pandita">Sakya Pandita</a> answered a summons from the Mongol prince <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/koten">Köden</a> and negotiated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tibet">Tibet's</a> submission. His nephew <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/drogon-chogyal-phagpa">Phagpa</a> became spiritual teacher to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kublai-khan">Kublai</a>, who made him Imperial Preceptor and in the 1260s gave the Sakya lamas authority over <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/tibet-1912-1951">Tibet</a>, organised after a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">census</a> into thirteen myriarchies.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The Sakya lamas spent much time at the Yuan court, while an administrator, the ponchen, governed from Sakya with Mongol backing; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shakya-zangpo">Shakya Zangpo</a> was the first. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/drogon-chogyal-phagpa">Phagpa</a> devised a new script for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kublai-khan">Kublai</a>'s empire in 1269. Rivalry with other monastic orders was intense, and a revolt by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/drikung-kagyu">Drikung</a> was crushed with Mongol troops in 1290.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>As Yuan power waned, the myriarch <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/changchub-gyaltsen">Changchub Gyaltsen</a> of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/phagmodrupa-dynasty">Phagmodru</a> fought the Sakya administrators and by 1354 controlled central <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/tibet-1912-1951">Tibet</a>, founding the Phagmodrupa dynasty. Sakya remained a major monastery and school of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tibetan-buddhism">Tibetan Buddhism</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/sakya-regime.jpg" alt="Sakya regime" width="320" height="258"><figcaption>Image: Jason22 and Chinyin · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Yuan_dynasty_and_Tibet.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1268 CE – 1354 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>theocracy</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>theocratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Sakya</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Phagpa, Shakya Zangpo</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Tibetan Buddhism (Sakya school)</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Tibetan</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/china">China</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Sakya dynasty, Sakya</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1311&amp;focus=sakya-regime" aria-label="Sakya regime on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="7399 1023 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Sakya regime lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M7867 1005h0M7559 780h0M7393 1364h0M7228 1376h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7719 1279h0"/><path class="map-dot-ap" d="M7719 1279h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>Location approximate: shown in present-day China. <a href="/atlas?year=1311&amp;focus=sakya-regime">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
